  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1157 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe seemingly never-ending hot and dry conditions will continue \nthrough Friday due to a persistent upper-level ridge over the Desert \nSouthwest and west Texas.  This ridge has 500mb heights of 594dam \nand 850mb temperatures of 23 to 26 deg C. These conditions, combined \nwith southerly winds and mostly sunny skies, should result in high \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to low 100s.  The highest mid-level \nheights will be located over northwest Texas and the Big Country. \nThere is a medium (40-70%) chance of temperatures reaching 105 \ndegrees or higher, which would meet heat advisory criteria.\n\nThe upper-level pattern looks to shift slightly for this weekend \nthrough Monday.  Models are in good agreement of a strong trough \ndropping down from the Upper Midwest to the Mid-Mississippi Valley \nby Saturday morning.  This feature should force the ridge westward \nand push a weak cold front southward through Texas by Sunday. \nEarlier deterministic runs hinted at some light rain chances for \nwest central Texas with this front, but more recent runs and the \nlong-range ensemble means have largely backed off on this. While \nheights should lower a bit, temperatures should only be a few \ndegrees cooler for Sunday into Monday.",
